@page "/help"
@inherits LocalizedComponentBase

@* The user guide, opened in its own window from the Home page's help button. The guide itself is in
   English only; the window's title and heading follow the chosen language. In-page links use "help#…"
   (not "#…"), as a bare fragment would resolve against the base address and go to the home page. *@

<PageTitle>@L.T("Help") · GS Point3D Web</PageTitle>

<div class="help-page">
    <header class="help-header">
        <h1>@L.T("Help")</h1>
        <span class="help-version">GS Point3D Web v@(AppVersion.Display)</span>
    </header>

    <div class="help-body">
        <nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Contents">
            <ol>
                <li><a href="help#start">Getting started</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#servers">Adding servers</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#list">The server list</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#views">Views</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#camera">Moving the model</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#front">What a view shows</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#settings">View settings</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#top">The top bar</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#network">Using it from other computers</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#files">Where things are stored</a></li>
                <li><a href="help#trouble">Troubleshooting</a></li>
            </ol>
        </nav>

        <article class="help-text">
            <p class="lead">
                GS Point3D Web shows telescope mounts as live 3D models that move as the mount does. It reads
                each mount through an ASCOM Alpaca server on the network, or through an ASCOM driver on the
                computer the app runs on, and works in any browser with WebGL.
            </p>

            <section id="start">
                <h2>Getting started</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li>Add a mount to the <strong>Telescope Mounts</strong> list on the left: search the network
                        with <strong>Alpaca Discovery</strong>, pick an <strong>ASCOM driver</strong>, or add one by
                        address (see <a href="help#servers">Adding servers</a>).</li>
                    <li>Drag the mount from the list onto a view on the right. It connects, and the model loads and
                        follows the mount.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>Everything you set up is saved, and is there the next time the app starts.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="servers">
                <h2>Adding servers</h2>
                <p>The buttons above the server list add mounts:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Add (+)</dt>
                    <dd>Adds a blank entry (<code>http://127.0.0.1:32323/api/v1/telescope/0/</code>). Double-click it
                        to give it a name and the mount's address.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery (magnifier)</dt>
                    <dd>Searches the local network for Alpaca telescopes (UDP port 32227) and lists the ones that
                        answer. Pick one to add it. A server on another network can't be found this way; add it by
                        address instead.</dd>
                    <dt>ASCOM Drivers (telescope)</dt>
                    <dd>Lists the ASCOM telescope drivers installed on the computer running the app. Only shown when
                        the app runs on Windows with the ASCOM Platform installed.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>
                    An Alpaca address looks like <code>http://host:port/api/v1/telescope/0/</code>, where the last
                    number is the device number on that server. An ASCOM driver's address is
                    <code>ascom:</code> followed by its ProgID, for example <code>ascom:ASCOM.Simulator.Telescope</code>.
                </p>
                <p class="note">Without a feature key, up to five servers can be added.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="list">
                <h2>The server list</h2>
                <p>
                    Click a server to select it. The dot beside it is green while it is connected, and the logo shows
                    whether it is an Alpaca server or an ASCOM driver. The other buttons act on the selected server:
                </p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Connect / Disconnect (plug)</dt>
                    <dd>Connects or disconnects the selected server.</dd>
                    <dt>Save (disk)</dt>
                    <dd>Saves the server list. Adding, editing and deleting servers also save it.</dd>
                    <dt>Delete (bin)</dt>
                    <dd>Removes the selected server from the list and from any view showing it.</dd>
                </dl>
                <h3>Editing a server</h3>
                <p>Double-click a server, or select it and press <kbd>F2</kbd>. You can change:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Server Name</strong>: the name shown in the list and on its views.</li>
                    <li><strong>Polling (seconds)</strong>: how often the mount is asked for its position,
                        0.5 to 5 seconds. Shorter is smoother; longer puts less load on the server.</li>
                    <li><strong>Url</strong>: the Alpaca address, or <code>ascom:&lt;ProgID&gt;</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>User Name</strong> and <strong>Password</strong>: only for an Alpaca server that
                        asks for them.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="views">
                <h2>Views</h2>
                <p>
                    The area on the right shows up to four views, each with its own mount. Choose how many with
                    <strong>Views 1 2 3 4</strong> in the top bar. A view that is hidden keeps its mount, and comes
                    back when the number is raised again.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Show a mount:</strong> drag a server from the list onto a view. It connects straight
                        away. Dropping a server on a view that already has one replaces it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Connect / Disconnect:</strong> the button at the top right of the model. After a
                        reload, or in another tab, views show their mounts without connecting; press
                        <strong>Connect</strong> to start.</li>
                    <li><strong>Remove:</strong> the <strong>×</strong> at the top of the view takes the mount off
                        that view. The server stays in the list.</li>
                    <li><strong>Settings:</strong> the gear turns the view over to its settings
                        (<a href="help#settings">View settings</a>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    The layout is shared: every browser tab or computer showing the app sees the same views and
                    mounts.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="camera">
                <h2>Moving the model</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>To</th><th>Mouse</th><th>Touch</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Turn the model</td><td>Drag with the left button</td><td>Drag with one finger</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Zoom</td><td>Scroll wheel</td><td>Pinch</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pan (move sideways)</td><td>Drag with the right button, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + left button</td><td>Drag with two fingers</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>
                    To keep a view you like, turn the card over and use <strong>Save current view</strong> under
                    <strong>Camera</strong>. Each view of that mount then opens at it.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="front">
                <h2>What a view shows</h2>
                <h3>Header</h3>
                <p>
                    The mount's name and, beside it, the model and mount type (for example
                    <em>Reflector · German polar</em>). The mount type is read from the mount when it connects.
                </p>
                <h3>Coordinates</h3>
                <p>Along the top of the model:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Ra</strong> and <strong>Dec</strong>: where the telescope points on the sky.
                        Double-click Ra to switch between hours and degrees.</li>
                    <li><strong>Az</strong> and <strong>Alt</strong>: the same position as azimuth and altitude.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lha</strong>: the local hour angle.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lst</strong>: the local sidereal time, always in hours.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Each one can be hidden in the view settings.</p>
                <h3>Mount state</h3>
                <p>
                    For servers that report it in one answer (<code>devicestate</code>: ASCOM Platform 7 and
                    ITelescopeV4 drivers), a strip along the bottom of the model shows <strong>Tracking</strong>,
                    <strong>Slewing</strong>, <strong>Parked</strong>, <strong>At home</strong> and
                    <strong>Guiding</strong> (a filled dot when on, a hollow one when off), the side of the pier, and
                    the mount's own UTC time. In a narrow view some of these are left out to make room.
                </p>
                <h3>Status bar</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>X, Y, Z</strong>: the mount's two axis angles and the tilt, as in GSServer.</li>
                    <li>The status: connecting, connected, or the last error.</li>
                    <li>A clock showing how long the view has been connected (hours:minutes:seconds).</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="settings">
                <h2>View settings</h2>
                <p>
                    The gear at the top of a view turns it over. Changes apply straight away and are saved with that
                    server, so every view of the same mount uses them. Press <strong>Done</strong> to turn back.
                </p>
                <h3>3D Model</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Model</strong>: the telescope drawn on the mount: reflector, refractor,
                        Schmidt-Cassegrain, Ritchey-Chrétien (tube or truss), dual telescope, or the default.</li>
                    <li><strong>Polar mode</strong>: which side of a polar (fork) mount the telescope is on.</li>
                    <li><strong>RA adjust</strong> and <strong>Dec adjust</strong>: turn the model's axes by a fixed
                        amount, if the model doesn't line up with the real mount. <strong>Reset adjustments</strong>
                        sets them back to zero.</li>
                    <li><strong>Mount</strong>: the mount type and latitude, read from the mount when it connects.</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>Appearance</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Model color</strong>: one of seven preset colours, or any custom colour.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ground grid</strong>: a grid on the ground under the mount.</li>
                    <li><strong>Background</strong>: a picture of the Heart nebula, the Rosette nebula, the Whirlpool
                        galaxy (M51) or the Triangulum galaxy (M33), or a plain dark sky.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reset appearance</strong> sets these back to their defaults.</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>Coordinates on the front</h3>
                <p>
                    Choose which coordinates show over the model, whether the <strong>mount state</strong> strip
                    shows, and whether Ra is shown in hours or degrees.
                </p>
                <h3>Camera</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Save current view</strong>: remembers how the model is turned, zoomed and panned on
                        the front.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reset to saved view</strong>: moves the camera back to the saved view (or the default
                        if none is saved).</li>
                    <li><strong>Clear saved view</strong>: forgets the saved view and uses the default.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="top">
                <h2>The top bar</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Version</strong>: the app's version, beside its name.</li>
                    <li><strong>Connected</strong>: how many servers in the list are connected.</li>
                    <li><strong>Log</strong>: opens the log in its own window. It can show only warnings and
                        errors, follow the log as it grows, and download the whole file.</li>
                    <li><strong>Help</strong> (?): opens this guide.</li>
                    <li><strong>Views</strong>: how many views to show (1 to 4).</li>
                    <li><strong>Language</strong> (globe): English, Deutsch, Español, Français, Italiano or 中文.
                        The choice is remembered by the browser.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="network">
                <h2>Using it from other computers</h2>
                <p>
                    By default the app only accepts connections from the computer it runs on, at
                    <code>http://localhost:5000</code>. To open it from other computers or a tablet, start it with
                    the address to listen on, then browse to that computer's name or IP address and port:
                </p>
                <pre><code>GS.Point3DWeb --urls "http://0.0.0.0:5000"</code></pre>
                <p class="warning">
                    Anyone who can reach that address can connect and disconnect your mounts and change the server
                    list, so only do this on a network you trust.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="files">
                <h2>Where things are stored</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Windows</th><th>Linux / macOS</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Server list and view settings (<code>servers.json</code>)</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\GS.Point3DWeb</code></td><td><code>~/.config/GS.Point3DWeb</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Feature key (<code>feature.key</code>)</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\GS.Point3DWeb</code></td><td><code>~/.config/GS.Point3DWeb</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Log files (<code>point3dweb-yyyyMMdd.log</code>; the last 14 are kept)</td><td><code>Documents\GS.Point3DWeb</code></td><td><code>~/.config/GS.Point3DWeb/logs</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>
                    If the Documents folder can't be written (for example because of Windows Security's Controlled
                    folder access), the logs go to <code>%APPDATA%\GS.Point3DWeb\logs</code> instead. To move your
                    setup to another computer, copy <code>servers.json</code>.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="trouble">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery finds nothing</dt>
                    <dd>Check the Alpaca server is running and has discovery turned on, and that a firewall isn't
                        blocking UDP port 32227. Discovery only reaches the local network; add other servers by
                        address.</dd>
                    <dt>A view won't connect</dt>
                    <dd>Check the address (double-click the server in the list) and that the server answers in a
                        browser at the same address. The view's status bar, and <strong>Log</strong>, show the
                        error.</dd>
                    <dt>The model is empty or says it failed to load</dt>
                    <dd>The browser needs WebGL. The view tries loading the model again by itself after a short
                        wait; reloading the page also works.</dd>
                    <dt>The model points the wrong way</dt>
                    <dd>Check the mount's site latitude in its driver, then use <strong>RA adjust</strong> and
                        <strong>Dec adjust</strong> in the view settings.</dd>
                    <dt>No mount state strip</dt>
                    <dd>The server doesn't report <code>devicestate</code> (older drivers), or the strip is turned off
                        in <strong>Coordinates on the front</strong>.</dd>
                    <dt>No ASCOM Drivers button</dt>
                    <dd>ASCOM drivers can only be used when the app runs on Windows with the ASCOM Platform
                        installed.</dd>
                    <dt>The page says it is rejoining the server</dt>
                    <dd>The browser lost touch with the app (it was closed or restarted, or the network dropped). The
                        page tries to rejoin by itself; if it can't, press <strong>Retry</strong> or reload the
                        page.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</div>
